      <a href="#pediatrician">When to Ask a Doctor</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          The best protein powder for teens is a plain, third-party certified whey or plant protein with nothing else in it: no stimulants, no creatine, no weight-gainer sugar load. Our top pick is Thorne Whey Protein Isolate, which is NSF Certified for Sport per the brand. But the more useful answer is that most teenagers do not need protein powder at all. National survey data consistently show that most American children and teens already meet the government's protein recommendation from ordinary meals, so a shake is a convenience for busy athletes and picky eaters, not a requirement for growth or performance. Food first, a certified powder second, and a conversation with the pediatrician before anything stronger.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="quick-comparison" class="scroll-mt-24">
