            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="do-teens-need-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Do Teens Actually Need Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Usually not, and it is worth being honest about that before spending any money. The US Dietary Reference Intakes set the protein recommendation for teenagers at a level that a normal diet clears without effort, and federal survey data have shown for years that average protein intake among American children and adolescents sits at or above that target. A teen who eats eggs or yogurt at breakfast, a sandwich with meat or beans at lunch, milk with meals and a regular family dinner is very likely covered already.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where powder earns a place is logistics, not biology. A sophomore with a 6 a.m. swim practice, a full school day and an evening lift may not have time to eat enough between sessions. A vegetarian teen whose family cooks mostly pasta and rice can fall short. A picky eater who refuses meat and dairy, or a teen whose appetite crashes during a growth spurt or heavy training block, may genuinely struggle to eat enough. In those cases a single shake is a reasonable bridge between meals, in the same way a glass of chocolate milk is.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also a cost beyond money. Shakes can quietly replace meals that would have brought iron, calcium, fiber and a long list of micronutrients that growing bodies need, and for some teens an intense focus on supplements is an early sign of a worrying relationship with food or body image. A powder should sit alongside regular meals, never instead of them. If you are deciding whether your teen needs one at all, start with our guide to <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a> and add up a normal day of their eating first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-much-protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Much Protein Do Teens Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The official numbers come from the Dietary Reference Intakes published by the National Academies. The Recommended Dietary Allowance for protein is 0.95 g per kilogram of body weight per day for children aged 4 to 13, and 0.85 g per kilogram for adolescents aged 14 to 18. Expressed as daily totals for a reference body size, that works out to about 34 g a day for ages 9 to 13, about 46 g a day for girls aged 14 to 18 and about 52 g a day for boys aged 14 to 18.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Protein RDA by Age (DRI)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">34 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Ages 9-13, per day</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">46 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Girls 14-18, per day</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">52 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Boys 14-18, per day</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0.85 g/kg</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">RDA, ages 14-18</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The RDA is set to cover the needs of nearly all healthy people in an age group, so it is a floor for adequacy rather than a target for performance. The same reference values also give an acceptable range for protein of 10 to 30 percent of total calories for ages 4 to 18. A 16-year-old eating 2,800 calories a day could get anywhere from 70 g to 210 g of protein and still sit inside that range, which shows how much room an ordinary diet leaves.
          </p>

          <p>
            Teen athletes in heavy training often need more than the RDA. Sports dietitians commonly work with intakes above the adult RDA for young athletes, frequently somewhere in the region of 1.2 to 1.8 g per kilogram, with the exact figure depending on the sport, training load and growth stage. For a 60 kg (132 lb) high school wrestler, that is roughly 72 to 108 g a day. That sounds like a lot until you count it: three eggs (about 18 g), a cup of Greek yogurt (about 20 g), a chicken sandwich (about 30 g), two glasses of milk (about 16 g) and a dinner portion of meat or tofu (25 g or more) already reach the top of that range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timing matters a little more than the total for an athlete who trains twice a day. Spreading protein across breakfast, lunch, an after-practice snack and dinner, at around 20 to 30 g each, is a sensible pattern. That after-practice slot is the one most often missed, because practice ends at an awkward time and dinner is two hours away. It is also the slot where a single shake or a bottle of milk makes the most practical sense. Our <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> gives an adult-oriented estimate; for a growing athlete, treat it as a rough guide and let a pediatrician or sports dietitian set the actual number.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="can-kids-have-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Can Kids Have Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Physically, yes. A plain whey or pea protein powder is a food ingredient, made from milk or peas, and a small amount in a smoothie is not dangerous for a healthy child. The better question is whether a child should have it, and for most younger children the answer is that there is no good reason to. Children under 13 have modest protein needs, around 19 g a day for ages 4 to 8 and 34 g a day for ages 9 to 13 at reference body weights, and a single scoop of an adult powder can deliver most or all of that in one go.
          </p>

          <p>
            The risks are mostly about what comes with the protein. Adult powders are formulated for adult body sizes and often contain sweeteners, gums, added vitamins at adult doses and, in the case of many sports blends, caffeine or creatine. Independent testing groups have also reported measurable lead and cadmium in a share of protein powders, more often in plant-based products, and a child's smaller body weight means the same scoop carries a proportionally larger exposure. None of that makes an occasional serving an emergency, but it is a poor trade when milk, eggs and yogurt deliver the same protein with fewer unknowns.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you do decide to use a powder for a younger child, keep it simple: a half scoop of an unflavored or lightly sweetened, third-party certified whey or pea protein blended into a fruit smoothie, a few times a week at most, alongside normal meals. Skip anything labeled pre-workout, mass gainer, fat burner or test booster entirely. Our explainer on whether <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder is safe</a> covers the contamination question in more depth.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="certifications" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Why Banned-Substance Certification Matters for Teen Athletes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dietary supplements in the United States do not need FDA approval before they go on sale. The manufacturer is responsible for safety and for the accuracy of the label, and enforcement mostly happens after a problem surfaces. For most adults that is a quality concern; for a teen athlete who may be drug tested at a state championship, a college showcase or under NCAA rules later on, it is a real eligibility risk, because athletes are generally held responsible for whatever is in their body regardless of how it got there.
          </p>

          <p>
            Contamination is not hypothetical. In a widely cited 2004 study in the International Journal of Sports Medicine, Geyer and colleagues analyzed 634 non-hormonal supplements bought in 13 countries and found that about 15 percent contained anabolic-androgenic steroids or prohormones that were not declared on the label. Protein powders are lower risk than pre-workouts or muscle builders, but they are often made in facilities that also produce those products, and cross-contamination is exactly the problem certification programs were built to catch.
          </p>

          <p>
            The two programs worth looking for are NSF Certified for Sport and Informed Sport, with Informed Choice as a related program from the same organization as Informed Sport. NSF Certified for Sport checks that the product contains what the label says, screens for contaminants and tests for a long list of substances banned in sport, and audits the manufacturing facility. Informed Sport tests every batch before release for banned substances. Neither is a guarantee, but both shift the odds substantially in a teen athlete's favor.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">VERIFY IT YOURSELF</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Check the exact product and flavor in the certifier's database.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A logo on a tub or a claim on a website is not the same as a current listing. Search NSF's Certified for Sport directory or the Informed Sport site for the brand, product and flavor before buying, and check again when you reorder. Certifications are granted per product and sometimes per flavor, and they can lapse.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            That is why the top four picks in this guide all carry NSF Certified for Sport per the brand, and why Optimum Nutrition, with its Informed Choice program, ranks above uncertified products despite a longer ingredient list. If your teen does not compete in tested sport, certification still has value as an independent check on label accuracy and contaminants. Our guide to the <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">best clean protein powder</a> explains what each certification does and does not test.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-avoid" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What to Avoid: Stimulants, Creatine Blends and Gainers
          </h2>

          <p>
            The protein itself is rarely the problem in a teen's supplement. The extras are. Many sports protein products now include caffeine, green tea extract, guarana or other stimulants, often marketed as energy or focus blends. A scoop can carry as much caffeine as a strong coffee, and a teen who also drinks energy drinks or soda can easily stack up an uncomfortable dose. The American Academy of Pediatrics has said that energy drinks have no place in the diets of children and adolescents, and a stimulant-laced shake is the same problem in powder form.
          </p>

          <p>
            Creatine is the other common add-in. It is one of the best researched supplements for adults, but the research base in adolescents is much thinner, and the American Academy of Pediatrics' 2016 clinical report on performance-enhancing substances discourages their use by adolescents, with creatine among the substances it covers. A protein powder that quietly includes creatine makes that decision for the family without asking. Check the supplement facts panel, not just the front of the tub, and look for words such as creatine monohydrate, HMB or any proprietary performance matrix.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mass gainers are a third category to skip unless a doctor or dietitian specifically recommends extra calories. A typical gainer serving can deliver 1,000 calories or more, much of it from maltodextrin and sugar, which is a lot of energy for a teen who simply wants a post-practice snack. Underweight teens who struggle to eat enough may need a calorie-dense approach, but that is best handled with food and professional guidance, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-weight-gain/">weight gain protein powder</a> guide is written for adults.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, avoid anything that promises hormonal effects. Products marketed as testosterone boosters, anabolic, natural steroid alternatives or with names that hint at the same are at the highest risk of containing undeclared drugs, and they are the products sports regulators warn about most often. A teenager's own hormones are already doing the heavy lifting during puberty. The safe rule is simple: protein, flavoring, sweetener and maybe a thickener or enzyme, and nothing else on the label.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How We Picked These Powders
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not run a teen taste test or send samples to a lab. These rankings are built on what can be checked: each product's supplement facts panel and ingredient list, the brand's stated certification status at the time of writing, typical retail cost per serving, and the published research on adolescent protein needs. Where we mention taste or mixing, that reflects patterns in buyer reviews rather than our own impressions. Our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test</a> page describes the method in full.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first filter was the formula. Anything with stimulants, creatine, test-booster ingredients, proprietary blends or a gainer-style carbohydrate load was excluded outright, which removed a large share of the sports protein aisle. We then ranked the remaining products on independent certification, putting NSF Certified for Sport products at the top because that program covers label accuracy and contaminants as well as banned substances.
          </p>

          <p>
            We included one ready-to-drink option and one uncertified budget powder on purpose. Core Power is closer to flavored milk than to a supplement and is easy for a teen to throw in a gym bag. Naked Whey has a single ingredient and a low cost per serving, which suits families whose teen does not compete in tested sport. For the wider market, including adult-focused picks, see our main <a href="/best-protein-powder/">best protein powder</a> rankings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How Teens Should Use a Protein Shake
          </h2>

          <p>
            One serving a day is enough for almost every teen who needs a shake at all. The best time is usually the gap that food cannot easily fill: straight after practice when dinner is still hours away, or with breakfast on a morning when there is no time to cook. A shake made with milk adds another 8 g or so of protein plus calcium and vitamin D, both of which matter for bone growth in the teenage years, so milk is a better base than water for most teens who tolerate dairy.
          </p>

          <p>
            A shake works best as part of a snack rather than the whole snack. A banana or a handful of cereal alongside a protein shake after practice adds the carbohydrate that refills muscle glycogen, which matters for a teen training again the next afternoon. For teens who prefer food, chocolate milk, a turkey sandwich or Greek yogurt with granola do the same job just as well, and our <a href="/best-protein-drink/">best protein drink</a> guide compares the ready-to-drink options if convenience is the main issue.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the scoop size honest. Teens often treat the scoop as a minimum and add a second for good measure. Two scoops of most powders means 40 to 50 g of protein in one sitting, which is more than a growing body can use well at once and adds 250 calories or more. Start with one level scoop, or even a half scoop in a smoothie for younger or smaller teens, and watch whether it actually replaces something missing or simply adds on top of an already adequate diet.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pediatrician" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. When to Check With a Pediatrician or Dietitian
          </h2>

          <p>
            This guide is general information, not medical advice, and parents should check with their teen's pediatrician before adding any supplement to a regular routine. That is especially important if the teen has a chronic health condition, takes medication, has food allergies, is trying to change weight quickly for a sport, or has kidney disease. A pediatrician can also look at growth charts and blood work in a way no label can.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some situations call for a specialist. A registered dietitian, ideally one who works with young athletes, can build a meal plan around training schedules and food preferences and often removes the need for a supplement entirely. Teens in weight-class sports such as wrestling, rowing or martial arts are a particular concern, because rapid weight cutting combined with supplement use carries risks that go well beyond protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for warning signs around food and body image. A teen who becomes preoccupied with protein grams, skips meals in favor of shakes, exercises compulsively or talks about their body in a distressed way may need support rather than a better supplement. Eating disorders and muscle dysmorphia often start during adolescence and affect boys as well as girls. Raising those concerns with a pediatrician early is far better than waiting.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the majority of healthy teens, the conversation will be short. The pediatrician will probably confirm that food covers their needs, that one certified protein shake on busy days is fine, and that anything with stimulants, creatine or hormonal claims is best avoided. That is exactly the approach this guide recommends.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a powder before checking the diet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most teens already meet the protein RDA from food. Add up a normal day of breakfast, lunch, snacks and dinner before buying anything, because a shake on top of an adequate diet mostly adds calories and cost. The gap, if there is one, is usually a single missed snack after practice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Picking a sports blend with stimulants</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many protein products now include caffeine, green tea extract or guarana. A teen who also drinks soda or energy drinks can stack up an uncomfortable dose. Read the supplement facts panel, not just the front label, and choose protein-only formulas without any energy blend.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a logo instead of the database</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A certification badge on a website is not the same as a current listing. Look up the exact product and flavor in the NSF Certified for Sport directory or on Informed Sport before buying, and check again when you reorder, because certifications are per product and can lapse.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Doubling the scoop for faster results</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Two scoops of most powders means 40 to 50 g of protein at once, more than a growing teen uses well in one sitting. Bigger doses do not speed up muscle growth during puberty; training, sleep and total food intake do. One level scoop, or half for younger teens, is plenty.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting shakes replace real meals</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A shake delivers protein but not the iron, fiber and range of nutrients that breakfast, lunch and dinner bring. Teens who swap meals for shakes risk falling short on calories and micronutrients during the years they need them most. Use a shake to bridge a gap between meals, not to skip one.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best protein powder for teens?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most teen athletes, a third-party certified whey isolate such as Thorne Whey Protein Isolate or Klean Athlete Klean Isolate, both NSF Certified for Sport per their brands. Dairy-free teens can use Garden of Life Sport Organic Plant-Based. Whatever you choose, it should be protein only, with no stimulants, creatine or gainer-style carbohydrate load, and the certification should be confirmed in the certifier's database.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can kids have protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  A healthy child can safely have a small amount of a plain whey or pea protein, but most do not need it. Children aged 9 to 13 need about 34 g of protein a day at reference body weights, which normal meals easily cover. Adult powders may carry sweeteners, adult-dose vitamins or stimulants, so check with your pediatrician first and favor milk, eggs and yogurt.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein does a teenager need per day?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The RDA is 0.85 g per kilogram of body weight for ages 14 to 18, which is about 46 g a day for girls and about 52 g for boys at reference body sizes. Teen athletes in heavy training often need more, frequently in the region of 1.2 to 1.8 g per kilogram, and a pediatrician or sports dietitian can set an individual target.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>At what age can you start taking protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  There is no official minimum age, because protein powder is a food product rather than a drug. In practice, most pediatric guidance favors food for younger children and reserves supplements for teens with a real gap or a medical reason. Many families wait until mid-teens and organized training, and talk to the pediatrician before starting a regular routine.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is whey protein safe for a 14-year-old?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Plain whey protein is a milk protein and is generally safe for a healthy 14-year-old in normal amounts, such as one scoop a day. The concerns are the extras, like stimulants and creatine, and contamination with banned substances. Choose a protein-only, third-party certified product, keep to one serving, and avoid it if your teen has a milk allergy or kidney disease.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Should teen athletes take creatine with their protein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not without a doctor's input. The American Academy of Pediatrics' 2016 clinical report on performance-enhancing substances discourages their use by adolescents, and creatine is among the substances it covers. The research base in teens is limited compared with adults. Avoid protein powders that include creatine, HMB or performance blends, and let a pediatrician or sports medicine physician weigh in.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can protein powder stunt a teenager's growth?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  There is no good evidence that a sensible amount of plain protein powder stunts growth. Growth problems are more likely when shakes replace meals and a teen ends up short on total calories, calcium or other nutrients, or when supplements contain hormonally active ingredients. Keep shakes alongside regular meals, choose a certified protein-only formula, and raise any growth concerns with your pediatrician.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

